<<~ aka lar:///ha.ka.ba/lares/api/pono/RFC-2119#/normative-language>>

<<^ code="&#x0002;">>

<<~ ahu #/head>>

! The Protective Optional

The Protective Optional names a compatibility shim built for a consumer that does not exist.

A required parameter would break existing callers, so the node makes it optional //so every existing caller keeps its behaviour//. In an early-alpha codebase with no outside consumers, the callers being protected are ''the node's own tests'' — and the hedge lands exactly where it costs most, because the unsafe default stays live wherever a later hand forgets.

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu #/ooda-ha>>

✶ detect an optional parameter, a default value, or a fallback added during a change.
⏿ orient by asking ''who does this protect'' — name them.
◇ decide: if the answer reads //my tests//, the parameter wants to be required.
▶ act — required, with a ''named honest-nothing'' where a caller genuinely holds nothing to pass (a value that DECLARES the absence at the call site, never an omitted argument nobody sees).
↺ let the break surface: requiring it SHOULD break witnesses, and each break names a place the default answered silently and wrongly.

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu #/law>>

In early alpha, ''an optional parameter is a question about who the node is protecting.''

The node MUST NOT add a compat shim for consumers that do not exist (`no-lean-in-early-alpha`).
The node SHOULD prefer a REQUIRED parameter plus a named absence-value over an optional one with a silent default.
The node MUST let a required parameter break its own witnesses rather than defaulting past them — the breaks name the sites where the default carried a wrong answer.

''The tell'': a silent default and a considered choice read identically at the call site. A named absence-value makes them differ, which is the whole of the cure.

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu #/diagnostic>>

If the parameter is optional and the only callers are witnesses, the Protective Optional occurred.
If the parameter is required and callers holding nothing pass a named absence, discipline held.
If the parameter is optional and a real outside consumer needs the default, that reads as compatibility rather than this failure — and early alpha holds none.

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu #/edges>>

